destination: Litterbox lets a user rewrite the content and history of a sandbox's branch. The sandbox adopts the rewrite when it has no unsaved work. A divergence is handed to the user on the host as a merge based on the revision both sides agreed on. Git and jj are supported to the limits of their capabilities.
created: 2026-11-18
status: open
settled: "Both Git and jj are supported behind one VCS trait; ConflictModel::{Native, Blocking}; Deferred/notes rejected; explicit `[vcs] tool` with per-VCS configuration sections in the untracked local override, recorded per sandbox; WorkspaceBinding::{Copied, Bound} with no default and legacy Copied; four-tree identity with `Workspace` measured against the sandbox branch; the sandbox adopts when clean and holds the branch when dirty; Git never merges in the sandbox and the host resolves in a linked worktree with `synced` as the base; staging is consent-gated and never writes the user's checkout; dropped tips stay in the branch reflog; MCP `workspace` block, `sandbox-status`, `sandbox-reconcile`; operator `status` and `reconcile`; `workspaces`/`synced` refs; jj non-colocated bridge, scoped abort, export surfaced; history is user configuration with no default."
next: "Frontier is empty — all 20 tickets are answered. T-018 proves Seatbelt masks a bound workspace by deny rule, so `Bound` is reachable on macOS. The Darwin masking change amends `adrs/2026-11-21-hide-vcs-metadata` rather than spawning a new ADR. Still outstanding: the VCS selector, `[vcs.jj]` and the jj bridge."
adr: adrs/2026-11-19-decouple-vcs-from-orchestration
decision: "Git and jj sit behind one VCS trait with opaque revisions and capabilities. Define `vcs` and `vcs::git_common` first, add arch-lint rules, adapt the existing Git implementation to the interface, build the plan surface on Git `Blocking`, and add jj later as a second implementation that uses `vcs::git_common` and never calls `vcs::git`. Git uses `Blocking` as a hold: the shared branch stays at the user tip while the sandbox snapshots its own branch, and the host resolves the difference. jj uses `Native` (conflicts in commits, one jj workspace per sandbox, non-colocated bridge, `.jj` masked). `Deferred` and git-notes emulation are rejected. The source state is derived from Base/Workspace/Merge axes over four trees, and `Workspace` compares the workspace with the sandbox branch. One `plan` is the only consumer. No sandbox tool is blocked while diverged; only the branch advance is held. Every sandbox tool returns a proactive `workspace` block; `sandbox-status` and `sandbox-reconcile` are the model surface, and `sandbox-reconcile` stages only. `status` and `reconcile` with `--finish` and `--abort` are the operator surface. Staging is a linked worktree under the data directory on a temporary branch; the merge uses `synced` as its base because Git's inferred base is wrong after a rebase. The user's checkout is never written. Dropped tips stay in the branch reflog; no new ref is minted. History retention and restore semantics are user configuration with no default; Git uses the reflog, jj uses its op log, and the user's repository is not the history store. Open risks: the masked `.jj` is never absent — an empty mountpoint under bubblewrap/Docker, present-but-inaccessible under Seatbelt's deny rule (T-018); the non-colocated jj bridge exposes only the Git remote, so a conflicted jj commit is not a supported Git consumer, export failures must surface, aborts must be scoped and bridge operations serialised; delete warns about unmerged work and `--force` only means stop it while running."
tickets[20]{id,question,blockers,state,answer,evidence}:
  T-001,"Can a jj repository be backed by a bare Git repository and host multiple jj workspaces, one per sandbox?",,answered,"Yes: a non-colocated jj repo backed by a bare Git repo hosts one jj workspace per sandbox; the bridge needs an initial workspace, and each sandbox branch must be a bookmark exported with `jj git export`.",evidence/T-001.md
  T-002,"Can a jj workspace be bind-mounted into a sandbox with `.jj` masked on bubblewrap, Docker and Seatbelt?",,answered,"Bubblewrap and Docker hide the contents of `.jj` with a tmpfs or empty volume; the directory entry remains empty; Seatbelt is unprobed on Linux; a backend that cannot mask must refuse `Bound`.",evidence/T-002.md
  T-003,"How does jj expose conflict paths and their base/ours/theirs sides, and does editing the materialised markers resolve the conflict or is `jj resolve` required?",T-001,answered,"Paths come from `jj resolve --list`; sides from `jj diff --from <base> --to <ours|theirs> --git -- <path>`; editing the materialised markers and snapshotting clears the working-copy conflict, so `jj resolve` is optional (merge-tool entry only).",evidence/T-003.md
  T-004,"Does `git merge-tree --write-tree --merge-base=<tree>` produce a usable conflict tree and exit status, and can that tree be committed and annotated as a conflicted commit?",,answered,"Yes: exit 1 on conflict, the tree OID is the first stdout line, the tree contains markers, and `commit-tree` yields an ordinary two-parent commit with no conflict metadata.",evidence/T-004.md
  T-005,"Can a conflicted Git commit be marked with refs/notes so Litterbox can compute inherited conflict for descendants, and what is the ref/note layout?",T-004,answered,"Technically yes, but no tool ecosystem uses notes for conflict or divergence; git-appraise and Gerrit use notes for review metadata only. Reject the pattern; Git uses `Blocking`, jj uses `Native`.",evidence/T-005.md
  T-006,"Can `rmcp` tool results carry a structured `workspace` block alongside the existing content, and what is the exact schema?",,answered,"Yes: `CallToolResult.content` is a `Vec<ContentBlock>` and `structured_content` is optional; append `ContentBlock::json(workspace)` or use `structured()`.",evidence/T-006.md
  T-007,"What is the exact `plan`/`decide` sequence for each action (tool, snapshot, rewrite, merge, resolve, abort, repair) and each conflict model?","T-001, T-003, T-005",answered,"One `plan` over Base/Workspace/Merge returns ordered steps; pre-tool and post-tool; no tool is blocked, but `Blocking` holds the snapshot and branch while `Native` snapshots and allows descendants; every tool returns the post-plan `workspace` block. T-019 supersedes the Git merge branch: a dirty sandbox blocks the advance instead of merging, and the host resolves.",evidence/T-007.md
  T-008,"How are the `workspaces`, `synced` and `conflicts` refs represented in jj and Git, and who updates them?","T-001, T-007",answered,"Git: branch plus `refs/litterbox/workspaces` and `synced`; history is a bridge reflog; `Blocking` merge sides in the record; no notes or conflict refs. jj: bookmark, synced bookmark or record field, jj workspace and op log, `jj git export`; abort is scoped, not a global op restore. T-019 fixes the `workspaces` ref as the sandbox branch and the `ours` side; T-020 fixes retention.",evidence/T-008.md
  T-009,"How do pause, missing, repair and delete interact with the source state, especially a conflicted sandbox?",T-007,answered,"Lifecycle and source are orthogonal; pause/resume do not touch the source; repair materialises the branch and re-materialises a `Blocking` merge from record sides; `--force` only means stop it while running, and delete warns about unmerged work against the recorded base.",evidence/T-009.md
  T-010,"What is the migration and configuration path for VCS selection and workspace binding on existing records?",,answered,"`[vcs] tool` with a per-VCS configuration section (`[vcs.git]`, `[vcs.jj]`) mirroring `[backends.*]`; the selected section supplies binding; required for create and stored per sandbox; legacy records are Git + Copied facts; incompatible combinations are refused at validation, never defaulted. The section is a workspace setting in the untracked `.agentkit/litterbox/config.local.toml`, not machine-wide user configuration: a machine hosts many workspaces, so one user file cannot carry their choices. The committed project file cannot set it.",evidence/T-010.md
  T-011,"What is the operator CLI surface, and how does it share the `plan` logic with MCP?",T-007,answered,"`status`, `conflicts`, `resolve`, `abort`, `shell`; every command calls the same orchestrator `plan`; no `sync` command (reconciliation is lazy); `--json` mirrors the MCP `workspace` block. T-019 supersedes the Git commands: `status` and `reconcile` with `--finish` and `--abort`; the sandbox never resolves.",evidence/T-011.md
  T-012,"How are `.jj`/`.git` masked per backend, and what does the backend do when it cannot mask a `Bound` workspace?",T-002,answered,"Bubblewrap and Docker mask `.jj` contents with a tmpfs or empty volume; the path remains as an empty mountpoint; a backend that cannot mask refuses `Bound`; Seatbelt is unprobed.",evidence/T-002.md
  T-013,"What is the current module layout, where is it Git-coupled, and what has to move for one VCS-neutral trait and a `plan`-driven orchestrator?",,answered,"Sandbox/backends are neutral; `scm` (1,777 lines) holds a Git-shaped trait and `GitScm`; `vcs_store` is a bare-Git store; `orchestrator/service.rs` (2,270) imports `ThreadSafeScm`/`VcsStore`/`ScmMode` and calls `git2::Repository::discover` directly; `domain::ScmError` wraps `git2::Error`; `mcp.rs` (2,276) and `cli.rs` (1,572) are monoliths. Define the VCS interface and `vcs::git_common` first, add arch-lint, adapt Git, build the plan/conflict surface, then add jj.",evidence/T-013.md
  T-014,"Should Git emulate deferred conflict resolution with notes/refs, given the lack of tool adoption?",T-005,answered,"No. Notes are a metadata pattern for review and attribution, not conflict state; Git uses `Blocking` and jj uses `Native`. `Deferred` is rejected.",evidence/T-005.md
  T-015,"How should multiple steps of sandbox history be kept without putting bookkeeping refs in the user's repository?",,answered,"Retention and restore semantics are user configuration with no default; Git uses a bridge reflog on `synced`, jj uses its op log; if `[history]` is absent, history/restore report unconfigured. The user's repository is not the history store. T-020: a dropped tip stays in the branch reflog; no new ref is minted.",evidence/T-015.md
  T-016,"What gaps does a non-colocated jj bridge open, and are they acceptable?",,answered,"C is accepted: only the Git remote is external, so a conflicted jj commit is not a supported Git consumer. Export the bookmark as-is, surface export failures, scope aborts, serialise bridge operations; no separate clean export.",evidence/T-016.md
  T-017,"What is the generic VCS interface, what code is shared between backends, and what arch-lint rules keep VCS code out of the orchestration code?",,answered,"Define `vcs` (trait, `Revision`, `TreeId`, `Capabilities`, `Merge`, `Conflict`, `ConflictSides`) and `vcs::git_common` (Git concepts: ignore, archive, object/ref primitives, path safety, naming) first; the contract must also express the operations orchestration uses today (branch create/delete, branch enumeration, archive, snapshot commit, snapshot filter, remote/direct fact); adapt Git; add jj later. Orchestrator imports `vcs` only (never `vcs::git_common`); `vcs::git` and `vcs::jj` may import `vcs`/`vcs::git_common` but never each other; arch-lint 0.11.0 scopes with `deny-scope-dep` and `independent` enforce the boundary.",evidence/T-017.md
  T-018,"Can Seatbelt deny a path inside a workspace it already allows, so a bound workspace can hide `.git` or `.jj`?",,answered,"Yes: SBPL resolves rules last-match-wins, so `(deny file-read* (subpath <ws>/.jj))` and `(deny file-write* (subpath <ws>/.jj))` emitted after the workspace allow rules override them; the `.jj` entry stays visible in a listing but every read, write and stat under it returns EPERM, and descendants inherit it.",evidence/T-018.md
  T-019,"Where does a rewritten branch get reconciled, and what does the sandbox do?",,answered,"The sandbox never merges. It adopts when its workspace matches its own branch and holds the shared branch at the user tip when it does not. The host resolves in a linked worktree on a temporary branch under the data directory. The merge uses the synced revision as its base because Git's inferred base is wrong after a rebase. Staging is a command the user runs or an MCP tool the harness may allow; it never writes the user's checkout. Finish fast-forwards when the branch is free and prints the command when the checkout holds it.",evidence/T-019.md
  T-020,"How are sandbox revisions that a rewrite drops retained?",,answered,"The branch reflog. Adoption reports the old tip once and names the reflog; the object stays reachable for the reflog lifetime. No new ref and no record field. The bare store logs every ref update; under direct the user's repository logs branches by default.",evidence/T-020.md
